For years, my go-to for Linux automation was simple: SSH in and run a series of commands. 
It felt efficient… until I realized I was fighting a losing battle.

The problem with raw Linux commands / Bash scripts:
Idempotency – Run the script twice and it often breaks things or creates duplicates. Most commands just execute; they don’t check the current state.
Error handling – If a command fails halfway, you’re left with a half-configured “zombie” server.

Enter Ansible
Ansible fixed both problems:
It is idempotent — it only changes what’s not already in the desired state.
It stops on failure, so you don’t cascade more damage.
It’s also agentless and provider-agnostic. Works on bare metal in your basement the same way it works on an EC2 instance. Prefer built-in modules (apt, yum, user, file, etc.) because they’re designed to be idempotent.

The escape hatch:
command module → safer, no pipes or redirection
shell module → full Bash power, but you become responsible for idempotency again

The missing piece: Terraform
Ansible is excellent at configuration (what happens inside the server).
Terraform is built for orchestration (creating the infrastructure itself).Biggest wins with Terraform:
Plan before apply — you see exactly what will change
Multi-cloud support with one syntax
Built-in dependency management (database before app server, etc.)
How I use them together
Terraform → creates the infrastructure (VMs, networks, load balancers)
Ansible → configures the software on top (Nginx, SSH, application code)

Takeaway
If you’re still writing long Bash scripts for critical automation, you’re probably creating technical debt.

Use Ansible for reliable, idempotent configuration.
Use Terraform when you need infrastructure as code.
What’s your current approach to Linux automation — pure Bash, Ansible, Terraform, or a mix?
